# Manufacturing Industries in India

> Manufacturing turns raw materials into finished goods and multiplies
> the value a country earns from its endowment. This lesson classifies
> Indian industries, locates the great clusters, and examines pollution
> control and the policy reforms reshaping the sector.

## 1. What Manufacturing Means and Why It Counts

[definition]
**Manufacturing** is the process of transforming raw materials into
more valuable finished goods with the help of machines, power, and
labour — literally "made by hand" in its root sense, though today it
means made by machine.
[/definition]

- Manufacturing adds value: raw cotton worth a few rupees becomes
  cloth worth hundreds, and ore becomes export-grade steel.
- It creates organised, comparatively well-paid jobs and spreads
  industrial skills; the sector contributes roughly one-sixth of
  gross value added, with policy aiming to raise that share.
- Industry creates demand for mining, energy, and transport, linking
  every economic sector into one chain.

## 2. Classifying Industries

@diagram tree id="industry-classification" layout="tree" aria="Basis-wise classification of industries"
  Industries -> "By raw material"
  Industries -> "By size"
  Industries -> "By ownership"
  "By raw material" -> "Agro-based: textiles and sugar"
  "By raw material" -> "Mineral-based: iron and steel, cement"
  "By raw material" -> "Forest and marine based"
  "By size" -> "Cottage and small scale"
  "By size" -> "Large scale"
  "By ownership" -> "Public, private, joint, cooperative"
@/diagram

[tip]
A **public sector** industry is owned by the government (SAIL steel
plants), a **private sector** one by individuals or companies (TISCO),
and a **cooperative** one by its members, such as the sugar
cooperatives of Maharashtra and Amul's dairy network.
[/tip]

## 3. Agro-Based Industries: Textiles and Sugar

**Cotton textiles** form India's oldest modern industry; the first
mill opened in Mumbai in 1854.

| Centre | Why it grew | Nickname |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mumbai | Humid climate, black-soil cotton hinterland, port and capital | Cottonopolis of India |
| Ahmedabad | Cheap labour and power beside the cotton tracts of Gujarat | Manchester of India |
| Coimbatore | Spinning and powerloom hub fed by Tamil Nadu cotton | Textile city of the south |

**Jute textiles** cluster in the Hugli basin of West Bengal, where
raw fibre, river water, cheap labour, and the Kolkata port lie side
by side. **Sugar mills** crush cane seasonally in Uttar Pradesh and
Maharashtra, and their by-products — bagasse, molasses, and press
mud — feed paper, ethanol, and cattle-feed industries; blending
ethanol with petrol is now a national programme.

[warning]
Mumbai's mills declined as land prices soared and machinery aged, so
the industry drifted to Gujarat, Tamil Nadu, and powerloom towns such
as Surat — industrial geography is never frozen in place.
[/warning]

## 4. Mineral-Based Industries: Steel, Aluminium, Cement, Automobiles

The first integrated steel plant — **TISCO at Sakchi (Jamshedpur),
1907** — still anchors a belt on the Chhotanagpur plateau, where iron
ore, coal, limestone, water, labour, and rail lines meet.

| Plant | State | Started |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Jamshedpur (TISCO) | Jharkhand | 1907 |
| Bhilai | Chhattisgarh | 1959 |
| Rourkela | Odisha | 1962 |
| Durgapur | West Bengal | 1962 |
| Bokaro | Jharkhand | 1972 |

- **Aluminium** smelters (NALCO at Koraput, Hindalco at Renukoot) sit
  near bauxite and cheap power because smelting is electricity-hungry.
- **Cement** plants track the limestone belts of Rajasthan, Andhra
  Pradesh, and Gujarat; the first plant opened at Chennai in 1904.
- **Automobiles** cluster at Chennai — dubbed the Detroit of India —
  and at Gurugram-Manesar, Pune, and Sanand.

[important]
Locational logic: weight-losing industries such as steel, sugar, and
cement pull toward raw materials, while weight-gaining or assembly
industries such as autos and electronics pull toward markets and
ports.
[/important]

## 5. Industrial Clusters and Location Factors

The classic location factors are raw material, power, market, labour,
transport, capital, water, and government policy.

| Cluster | Specialities |
| --- | --- |
| Mumbai-Pune | Textiles, machinery, pharmaceuticals, petrochemicals |
| Hugli region | Jute, engineering, paper, tea trade |
| Bengaluru-Chennai | Electronics, software, automobiles |
| Gujarat corridor | Chemicals, petrochemicals, textiles |
| Delhi NCR | Automobiles, electronics, light engineering |

[example]
Jamshedpur was sited where the rivers Subarnarekha and Kharkai meet,
close to Jharia coal and Singhbhum ore — every classic location
factor within a day's march.
[/example]

## 6. Industrial Pollution and Its Control

| Pollution type | Typical source | Control measure |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Air | Smoke and sulphur dioxide from smelters | Scrubbers, filters, taller stacks, cleaner fuels |
| Water | Dyes and effluents from tanneries and paper mills | Effluent treatment plants before discharge |
| Thermal | Hot cooling water into rivers | Cooling ponds and towers |
| Noise and solid waste | Machinery, fly ash | Barriers, recycling, ash bricks |

## 7. Policy Reforms and New Directions

- Industrial licensing was largely abolished in **1991**, opening the
  sector to competition and foreign investment.
- Special Economic Zones (SEZ Act, 2005) offer export-ready estates;
  **Make in India** (2014) and Production-Linked Incentive schemes
  push electronics, mobiles, autos, and pharmaceuticals.
- Micro, small, and medium enterprises (MSMEs) employ the largest
  number of people after agriculture and feed big-firm supply chains.

